Wave 15, lane D -- do not build a network server to start a local game.

MEASURED, from Forge's own crash dialog rendered in the browser
($SPIKE_CACHE/shots/43-after-start.png): clicking Start on a local Constructed
game against the AI dies with

  java.lang.UnsatisfiedLinkError: Java_sun_nio_ch_EPoll_eventSize
    at sun.nio.ch.EPoll.<clinit>
    at sun.nio.ch.EPollSelectorImpl.<init>
    at sun.nio.ch.EPollSelectorProvider.openSelector
    at io.netty.channel.nio.NioEventLoop.openSelector
    at io.netty.channel.nio.NioEventLoop.<init>
    ...
    at forge.gamemodes.net.server.FServerManager.<init>
    at forge.gamemodes.net.server.FServerManager.getInstance
    at forge.gamemodes.match.HostedMatch.startGame

This is the same class of blocker Wave 14 found on the CLIENT transport
(Java_sun_nio_ch_EPoll_create) and it was not known to be on the local-match
path as well. It is, and the reason is small: FServerManager's two Netty
EventLoopGroups are FIELD INITIALISERS, so merely constructing the singleton
opens epoll selectors -- and HostedMatch.java:183 constructs it only to ask
`isHosting()`, which for a local game is false. InputPassPriority.java:75 does
the same during play.

Constructing a network server in order to ask whether a network server is
running is a bug on any platform; under CheerpJ it is fatal. Making the groups
lazy fixes every caller at once rather than one call site at a time -- the same
argument as patch 0003, and the same reason lane B stopped patching
StorageReaderFolder callers one by one.

Behaviour is unchanged on a real JVM: startServer() creates the pair if absent,
stopServer() shuts down what exists and drops the references, and the next
startServer() makes fresh ones -- which is exactly what the "create new
EventLoopGroups for potential restart" line did, only deferred.

This patch does NOT make Forge's networking work under CheerpJ. Nothing here
gives sun.nio.ch natives to anybody; a hosted or joined network game still
cannot run. It only stops a LOCAL game from paying for a server it never uses,
which is what lane A's transport replacement exists to supersede.

